beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"cc49bf70bbb55964e1b44778e42aadb39ea37d24e6b9de4d04b907792e303ee2"},"seconds":56.677284240722656,"seed":1147403929563265339,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the rightmost non-zero digit in the expression\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001),\n\\]\nwe start by factoring and simplifying the expression.\n\nRewrite the expression:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001).\n\\]\n\nGroup the terms to factor out common factors:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101 - 1001) + (989899)(1001001 - 1001).\n\\]\n\nSimplify the terms inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n1010101 - 1001 = 1000100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1001001 - 1001 = 1000000.\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(1000000).\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the last non-zero digit of this expression. Let's consider the last digits of each term:\n- The last digit of \\(1001001\\) is \\(1\\).\n- The last digit of \\(1000100\\) is \\(0\\).\n- The last digit of \\(989899\\) is \\(9\\).\n- The last digit of \\(1000000\\) is \\(0\\).\n\nThus, the last digits of each product are:\n- The last digit of \\(1001001 \\times 1000100\\) is \\(0\\) (since it ends in \\(0\\)).\n- The last digit of \\(989899 \\times 1000000\\) is \\(0\\) (since it ends in \\(0\\)).\n\nSince both products end in \\(0\\), the last non-zero digit of the entire expression is determined by the digits before the trailing zeroes. We can ignore the trailing zeroes and focus on the non-zero digits.\n\nTo find the non-zero digit, we can consider the expression modulo 10 (ignoring the trailing zeroes):\n\\[\n(1001001 \\times 1000100) + (989899 \\times 1000000) \\equiv 1 \\times 0 + 9 \\times 0 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{10}.\n\\]\n\nSince the expression ends in \\(0\\) and we need to find the rightmost non-zero digit, we look at the expression modulo 9 (to find the non-zero digit):\n\\[\n1001001 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{9}, \\quad 1000100 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{9}, \\quad 989899 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{9}, \\quad 1000000 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}.\n\\]\nSo the expression modulo 9 is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 2 + 8 \\times 1 = 6 + 8 = 14 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{9}.\n\\]\n\nThe rightmost non-zero digit is \\(5\\) and it is followed by \\(3\\) zeroes (since the expression is a multiple of \\(1000\\)).\n\nThus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(5, 3)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(5, 3)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the rightmost non-zero digit in the expression\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001),\n\\]\nwe start by factoring and simplifying the expression.\n\nRewrite the expression:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001).\n\\]\n\nGroup the terms to factor out common factors:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101 - 1001) + (989899)(1001001 - 1001).\n\\]\n\nSimplify the terms inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n1010101 - 1001 = 1000100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1001001 - 1001 = 1000000.\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(1000000).\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the last non-zero digit of this expression. Let's consider the last digits of each term:\n- The last digit of \\(1001001\\) is \\(1\\).\n- The last digit of \\(1000100\\) is \\(0\\).\n- The last digit of \\(989899\\) is \\(9\\).\n- The last digit of \\(1000000\\) is \\(0\\).\n\nThus, the last digits of each product are:\n- The last digit of \\(1001001 \\times 1000100\\) is \\(0\\) (since it ends in \\(0\\)).\n- The last digit of \\(989899 \\times 1000000\\) is \\(0\\) (since it ends in \\(0\\)).\n\nSince both products end in \\(0\\), the last non-zero digit of the entire expression is determined by the digits before the trailing zeroes. We can ignore the trailing zeroes and focus on the non-zero digits.\n\nTo find the non-zero digit, we can consider the expression modulo 10 (ignoring the trailing zeroes):\n\\[\n(1001001 \\times 1000100) + (989899 \\times 1000000) \\equiv 1 \\times 0 + 9 \\times 0 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{10}.\n\\]\n\nSince the expression ends in \\(0\\) and we need to find the rightmost non-zero digit, we look at the expression modulo 9 (to find the non-zero digit):\n\\[\n1001001 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{9}, \\quad 1000100 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{9}, \\quad 989899 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{9}, \\quad 1000000 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{9}.\n\\]\nSo the expression modulo 9 is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 2 + 8 \\times 1 = 6 + 8 = 14 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{9}.\n\\]\n\nThe rightmost non-zero digit is \\(5\\) and it is followed by \\(3\\) zeroes (since the expression is a multiple of \\(1000\\)).\n\nThus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(5, 3)}\\).","id":"algebra:117"}
